steps.prompt:
The chat should show visuals the agent asks for, drawn with the design system’s new flow and map components, following the architecture’s decision on visuals in the chat. Design how they look in the transcript:

- a flow in the agent’s message, and a map with the parts a change touched;
- several visuals in one message, mixed with text;
- a block Prelude couldn’t read (unknown part id, wrong format): shown as the agent wrote it, with a quiet note saying why;
- a narrow chat column and the pop-out window;
- while the agent is still writing the block.

Add transcript states for each, and rules for what’s checked and what happens when it fails. Each part on a visual links to its page in the architecture. These transcript views are also the ask and fix conversation’s: cover its width too.

Asks and fixes should use the chat’s new visuals, as Design Workspace shows them. Design:

- an ask about how something works, answered with a flow through the real parts, each linking to its architecture page;
- an ask that ends with what it would change: a map with the parts it would reach, marked “would change”, above the list of files;
- a fix on its branch: the cause explained with a flow before changing anything, and after each turn the map of the parts it touched, beside the check’s findings, as in Build;
- fixing it on a branch: the visuals from the ask stay in the conversation as they were.

Add states for each, and rules for when the agent should draw (and when a few lines of text are enough). Keep the chat at its readable width.

Build sessions should help the developer see what’s being built and where it fits. Using the transcript visuals from Design Workspace, design:

- after each build turn, a map of the parts the turn touched, shown by Prelude without the agent asking, beside the check’s findings;
- when the agent should draw a flow itself: when it starts a story, when a change crosses parts, when it proposes a decision, written as rules the build agent is told;
- how a visual ties to the story or page being built.

Keep the stand-in transcript in step with the app’s. Add states: a turn touching one part, one touching several layers, and one with a drift finding.

Build Design Workspace’s visuals in the chat, following the architecture’s decision:

- the parser and checker in the part the decision names;
- one Eta partial per visual under server/views/, rendering the design system’s markup unchanged;
- the vocabulary in the agent’s prompts in server/agent.js, for every kind whose rules say it may draw;
- the series tokens and new components ported to the system Prelude’s pages use.

Unreadable blocks fall back as the rules say. Add tests for the parser: a good flow, an unknown part, a bad format, an unfinished block mid-stream.
